window.docContents[132].resolve({"/ISO-80000-5-___-Thermodynamics/The-energy-family___-distinguishing-kinds-by-defining-construction/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-5-___-Thermodynamics--The-energy-family___-distinguishing-kinds-by-defining-construction":{"contents":"ISO 80000-5 item 5-20 lists energy <thermodynamics> (5-20.1) as a genus and the\nthermodynamic potentials — internal energy (5-20.2), enthalpy (5-20.3), the Helmholtz\nenergy (5-20.4), and the Gibbs energy (5-20.5) — as items beneath it. Every one has\ndimension M·L²·T⁻² and ratio scale. The standard distinguishes them only in prose, by\nwhich thermodynamic variables are natural to the potential: internal energy is a state\nfunction of S, V, N; the enthalpy is H = U + pV; the Helmholtz energy is A = U − TS;\nthe Gibbs energy is G = H − TS. A dimension-only model sees one type, \"a real number of\njoules\", for all of them — and cannot even separate them from a Part-4 torque.\n\nThis is requirement R2 again, now on the thermodynamic potentials — the pattern Part 3\nset on the length family and Part 4 on the force family. Item 5-20.1 is the broad genus;\neach later item is a species of energy, distinguished not by fiat but by an explicit\ndefining construction (Dybkær's examination principle, §7.5). The species are then\nprovably distinct kinds while remaining mutually comparable as energies.\n\n\n\nAn energy species is a ratio-scale quantity-kind of dimension\nM·L²·T⁻² carrying an examination principle — this work's own\nterse descriptor of the defining construction that distinguishes it (natural in S-V-N,\nin S-p, in T-V, in T-p), never the standard's normative definition. Internal energy, the\nenthalpy, and the Helmholtz and Gibbs energies are all species of the general energy kind\n(item 5-20.1).\n\nenergySpecies id p := { kind := { id, scale := .ratio, examPrinciple := some p.id },\ndim := TDim.energy }. The general energy carries no examination principle; the\ndirect-parent edges of the family are collected in an Edge relation, each species an\nedge to energy (mirroring the standard's \"energy (item 5-20.1) … given by …\").\n\nThe Helmholtz and Gibbs energies are distinct kinds — by defining construction, not by\nfiat. They share dimension M·L²·T⁻² and are both \"free energies\", yet are different\nkinds because they are defined by different natural variables (A = U − TS, natural in\nT,V; G = H − TS, natural in T,p), proved through distinct_of_examPrinciple (§7.5). This\nis the thermodynamic-potential analogue of width ≠ distance and static ≠ kinetic friction\nforce. Uses the examination principle.\n\nhelmholtzEnergy_ne_gibbsEnergy : helmholtzEnergy.kind ≠ gibbsEnergy.kind, from\nKindOfProperty.distinct_of_examPrinciple applied to the differing principles natural-T-V\nand natural-T-p (a decide on the Option String links). The companions\ninternalEnergy_ne_enthalpy and internalEnergy_ne_energy separate the other species, and\ninternalEnergy_examinedBy checks the kind-to-principle link. Axiom-free.\n\nComparability is preserved. The Helmholtz and Gibbs energies, though distinct kinds,\nremain mutually comparable — they share the super-kind energy, so combining them is\npossible but only via an explicit up-cast, never silently. The companion\ninternalEnergy_specializes_energy records that internal energy specializes energy. Uses\nspecialization.\n\nhelmholtzEnergy_gibbsEnergy_comparable : MutuallyComparable Edge helmholtzEnergy.kind\ngibbsEnergy.kind, with witness energy.kind, each species reaching it by one edge.\nAxiom-free.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009ISO 80000-5 — Thermodynamics","header":"23.3. The energy family: distinguishing kinds by defining construction","id":"/ISO-80000-5-___-Thermodynamics/The-energy-family___-distinguishing-kinds-by-defining-construction/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--ISO-80000-5-___-Thermodynamics--The-energy-family___-distinguishing-kinds-by-defining-construction"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L3-___-The-body-opens-up/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Questions-to-ask-the-model-so-far":{"contents":"* Question* Check* What the verdict means\n* What bears the mass of body A's left half?* opened_body* Composite.part (HalfOf .P .A) bears it; the body whole bears the assembled total.\n* Can another record of body A in pair P use the same bearer?* opened_body* Yes; it is another state, configuration, or estimate of the named system.\n* Can pair Q's left-half mass be mixed with pair P's?* opened_body* No; nominal pair identities produce distinct bearer types.\n* How does this state's centre of mass depend on its halves?* center_of_mass_mean* centerOfMassCarving uses the actual half masses; 3 at 0 and 5 at 10 yield 25/4 rather than 5. Position addition is unlicensed.\n* What happens when total mass is zero?* massless_center_of_mass* PhysLib returns the origin by convention, but hypothesis h fails, so the state cannot construct a weighted carving.\n* Does angular velocity add over a carving?* angular_velocity_intensive* No; it is intensive, and the attempted assembly is refused.\n* Can the body inertia tensor contract with lab-frame ω?* bare_frame_mismatch, framed_rotational_energy* The bare PhysLib API accepts it, but the PKC frame index refuses it.\n* Where does the homogeneous-body assumption survive?* solid_sphere* As the solid-sphere theorem (2/5) m R² · 1, ready to contribute upstream.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L3 — The body opens up","header":"5.4.9. Questions to ask the model so far","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L3-___-The-body-opens-up/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Questions-to-ask-the-model-so-far"},"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L3-___-The-body-opens-up/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Synopsis":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L3 — The body opens up","header":"5.4.1. Synopsis","id":"/Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols/L3-___-The-body-opens-up/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L3-___-The-body-opens-up--Synopsis"},"/Uncertainty-quantification-and-numerical-adequacy/Conformity-assessment-_LPAR_ISO___IEC-Guide-98-4___-JCGM-106___2012_RPAR_/#conformity-assessment":{"contents":"A coverage interval says how far the measurand may lie from its estimate. It does not say what to\ndo with that at a limit, and that gap is where guard bands come from. The decision rule \"accept\niff the estimate is below the limit\" ignores the uncertainty entirely and runs a consumer's risk of\nessentially 50 % at the limit; every practitioner therefore applies a margin, and the margin is\nalmost always a chosen factor rather than a derived one. A chosen factor cannot be checked,\ncannot be shown to be too tight or too loose, and cannot shrink as evidence accumulates, so it\ncharges the first measurement's ignorance forever. ISO/IEC Guide 98-4 (JCGM 106:2012) is the\nstandard that closes the gap, and Conformity.lean is its executable face — Mathlib-free and\nFloat-carried like the Evidence module it builds on — with ConformityLadder.lean supplying,\nover ℝ, the argument that a margin derived this way may safely tighten itself. No requirement of\nthe R1–R28 catalogue claims this layer; it sits beside R18 as what a coverage factor is for once\na limit is in view.\n\nJCGM 106's vocabulary, as the module carries it. The tolerance limit  (§3.3.4) is the\nspecified bound on permissible values of the measurand — given, not computed. The acceptance\nlimit  (§3.3.8) is the bound the decision actually compares against. The guard band\n (§3.3.10), applied inward, is guarded acceptance: the acceptance interval sits inside\nthe tolerance interval and the consumer's risk falls; applied outward it is guarded rejection. The\nspecific consumer's risk  (§9.5.2) is, for a measured value  with standard\nuncertainty , the probability that the measurand is past  anyway; its counterpart, the\nspecific producer's risk, is the probability that a value rejected at  would in fact have\nconformed. Nothing here is a naked number: the limits are Quantity k values of the measurand, a\nprobability and a coverage factor ride distinct dimension-one kinds, so reading 1.96 as 0.95\nor comparing an estimate against a limit of another kind is a type error rather than a review\nfinding.\n\nTolerance limits as roles. A Tolerance k R is an optional LowerBound k R and an optional\nUpperBound k R — the directional roles of Bounds.lean, not bare endpoints — so each limit can\nonly ever be used from its own side, and an absent bound is none rather than an infinity.\nTolerance.admits asks whether a value conforms (the question a measurement cannot answer, which\nis why the rest of the module exists), and Tolerance.guardedBy derives the acceptance limits: it\nsubtracts a band from an upper limit and adds it to a lower one. That direction is forced by the\nrole, not chosen by convention, so \"applied the guard band the wrong way and turned guarded\nacceptance into guarded rejection\" is not a mistake a caller can make while still typechecking.\n\nRealized: Conformity.Tolerance with atMost/atLeast/ofIcc constructors (scale-gated by\nOrderKind, since a limit presupposes an order to be on one side of), Tolerance.admits through\neach endpoint's own directional decider, and Tolerance.guardedBy gated by DifferenceKind, the\nmetrological licence to form the difference at all.\n\nThe guard band , a quantity of the measurand's own kind. It is built through\nQuantity.mul on expansionLaw, so the kind of the product is derived from a stated law — a\ncoverage factor is dimension one in the role of expanding an uncertainty — rather than asserted.\nThe input is a Dispersion and the output is not: a guard band is an expanded uncertainty, but\nwhat it is used as is a displacement of a limit, and the change of role happens once, here.\naccepts is then the decision — accept iff the estimate lies within the\nguarded acceptance limits — with the estimate and its uncertainty at\ntheir roles, so the two cannot be exchanged into an inverted rule that guards enormously on a\nsmall uncertainty and not at all on a large one. assess packages the whole thing: an evidence\nrecord, a tolerance and a target risk in; the factor, the band, the acceptance limits, the\ndecision and the risk actually run out — the shape that makes the guard band an output.\n\nRealized: Conformity.guardBand as Dispersion.expanded unwrapped to a Quantity,\nConformity.accepts over Tolerance.guardedBy, consumerRisk/producerRisk/riskAtLimit as the\nrisks actually run, and Conformity.assess returning none — a refusal, not a factor nothing\njustifies — when the evidence cannot support a coverage factor at the target risk.\n\nThe band from a stated risk. factorForRisk inverts §9.5.2: to hold the specific consumer's\nrisk at a one-sided acceptance limit to , the factor is  — none unless\n, since a risk of zero demands an infinite band and a risk of one is not a decision\nrule. The factor stops being an input and becomes a consequence of a risk the operator names.\nriskForFactor is its exact inverse, , and prices a band somebody else chose.\nBoth are Gaussian, which is exact only when  is known; factorForEvidence and\nriskForFactorAt are the same pair at the evidence's own  (a Student  that is the\nGaussian at , so the correction appears and disappears by itself), and\nfactorForRiskRectangular/riskForFactorRectangular are the closed-form pair for GUM 4.3.7's\nbounded posterior, which saturates at  where the risk is exactly zero.\n\nRealized: Conformity.factorForRisk and riskForFactor through Sampling.normalQuantile and\nnormalCDF; riskForFactorAt through Sampling.studentTCDF, returning none when  is\nNaN or below one (fewer than two indications support no statement about a tail — the same\nrefusal Evidence.coverageFactorOneSided makes, not a Gaussian fallback); and the rectangular pair\naround rectangularFactorLimit. The direction in which a small  is dangerous is\nthe pricing one: at  a band of  buys , not the\n a Gaussian reads off it.\n\nThe risk from the costs, when nobody knows what risk to ask for. Accepting a non-conforming value\ncosts ; rejecting a conforming one costs ; at a measured value the expected costs are\n and , so accepting is the cheaper action exactly while\n, and that break-even risk is the target to hand to\nfactorForRisk. The two costs must be quantities of one kind, and the\nstep from their ratio to a probability is an authored crossing — nothing about a ratio of two\ndurations makes it a probability except the decision-theoretic argument. Strongly asymmetric costs\nthen produce the conclusion that is usually asserted: a wrong acceptance a hundred times as costly\nas a wrong rejection gives  and a one-sided , a derivation auditable in\na way \"we used 0.9\" is not.\n\nRealized: Conformity.riskFromCosts, dividing through costRatioIsRisk kc hc, none unless both\ncosts are positive numbers.\n\nReading a band that already exists. readBand g u divides a deployed margin by the measurand's\nown standard uncertainty — a quotient of two k-quantities, which bandReadingLaw declares to be\na coverage factor — and says what kind of thing the band is. A BandReading is one of three\nconstructors. coverage factor risk: a band at a few  is a coverage statement, dispersion that\nshrinks as evidence accumulates, and the risk it buys is attached. systematic factor: a band at\ntens of  is not — no risk target produces it under any posterior, so the margin is standing in\nfor a systematic the model does not carry, which does not shrink with evidence, and tightening it\nas though it did is how a fleet walks into a correlated failure. unstated: the band cannot be read\nat all. The two readable cases have opposite repairs, which is why the distinction is a constructor\nand not a comment; systematicThreshold (, where the Gaussian risk reaches ) is the\njudgement they turn on, taken as an argument so that it applies uniformly. Evidence.readBand e g\nis the same reading taken against a record's own evidence, priced at its  through\nriskForFactorAt instead of at the Gaussian limit, and declared in\nEvidence's namespace so the  cannot be dropped at the call site. BandReading.label\nforgets the evidence to a BandReadingLabel — the three-valued classification a report line or a\nrecord field states.\n\nRealized: Conformity.BandReading, BandReadingLabel, BandReading.label, systematicThreshold,\nreadBand (pricing with riskForFactor) and Evidence.readBand (pricing with riskForFactorAt\nat e.dof), both one-line calls into classifyBand. The band is a\nplain Quantity and the u a Dispersion, which is what stops  from being transposed into\n — both well-kinded coverage factors, and the transposition turns a  systematic into\n of a  that every downstream rule would happily shrink.\n\nThe one band-classification rule, and the NaN rule inside it.\nclassifyBand g u hk systematicAt price is the single place a band is classified: the guard\n( must be a positive number), the quotient  through bandReadingLaw, the comparison\nagainst the systematic threshold, and the pricing of a coverage statement through the price\nfunction the caller supplies — readBand passes the Gaussian riskForFactor, Evidence.readBand\npasses riskForFactorAt at the record's own . Nothing else differs between the two entry\npoints of the band reading.The NaN rule. A coverage factor that is not a number — , an unbounded band over\nan unbounded  — reads as unstated, and so does any band read against a systematic threshold\nthat is not a number. The rule cannot be left to the comparison: NaN ≤ t and NaN > t are both\nfalse, so a rule written \" ⇒ coverage, else systematic\" and the same rule written\n\" ⇒ systematic, else coverage\" classify the same band in opposite directions — one as a\nsystematic that must never be tightened, the other as a coverage statement every downstream rule\nmay shrink. Neither is right. unstated is chosen over the conservative systematic because a\nsystematic is a positive claim — a term the model does not carry — that a factor nobody can price\ndoes not support; and over the permissive coverage because that is the reading whose repair,\ntightening, is the dangerous one. A factor nobody can price is not a reading, and a threshold nobody\nstated classifies nothing. Because the guard lives in classifyBand and both entry points are\none-line calls to it, no caller can re-derive the wrong direction; and for every factor and\nthreshold that are numbers the rule is unchanged — the guard falls through to exactly the\ncomparison it always made, so  is still a systematic and the threshold itself is still\na coverage statement.\n\nRealized: Conformity.classifyBand, with the isNaN guards on the threshold and on the quotient\nahead of the > comparison, and none from price (a  nothing qualifies) also read as\nunstated. The checked facts are the probes of PropertyKindCalculus.Tests.Uncertainty.Evidence,\neach a #guard that fails the build if a reading moves:  reads unstated through\nreadBand and through Evidence.readBand at  — the case where a second copy of the\nrule had disagreed;  under a NaN threshold reads unstated through both; \nreads systematic;  and  read coverage (at  under the\nGaussian,  at ) and  reads systematic through both; the\nthreshold  itself reads coverage at the Gaussian limit and at ; and a  of\nzero, or a  qualified by , reads unstated.\n\nWhy an acceptance limit may be allowed to tighten itself. Deriving the band from a stated risk and\npooling evidence so that  falls gives a deployment that gets less conservative the longer it\nruns — exactly the automatic loosening that should not be allowed near anything that can be killed\nfor using too much memory. Over ℝ, with the guarded upper acceptance limit :\nthe limit rises as  falls and never past , so the mechanism is a ratchet with a ceiling;\nand — the whole content — the specific consumer's risk at the acceptance limit is  for\nevery , independent of , because band and uncertainty shrink together and the\nstandardized deviate they are read at is exactly . A rule built for a target risk therefore\nruns that target at every stage of evidence, the cold start included: with one Type B prior the\nloop is safe and merely inefficient. What is not claimed: that the risk is unchanged by evidence is\na statement about the rule, not about the world — if the pooled  is wrong, a\nsystematic reading is the one thing the loop must never tighten,\nand a floor on  is the operational guard the theorem leaves to the deployment.\n\nRealized over ℝ in ConformityLadder: acceptanceLimit_antitone and acceptanceLimit_le (the\nratchet and its ceiling), riskAt_acceptanceLimit (the invariance, by the algebra\n, with the tail  an arbitrary function), riskAt_acceptanceLimit_le (the\ntarget survives every update of ), riskAt_le_of_accepted and\nriskAt_acceptanceLimit_antitone_in_factor (needing only Antitone Q, which the Gaussian, \nand rectangular tails all satisfy), and acceptanceLimit_le_of_floor (a floor on  is a floor\non the limit).\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Uncertainty quantification and numerical adequacy","header":"19.8. Conformity assessment (ISO/IEC Guide 98-4, JCGM 106:2012)","id":"/Uncertainty-quantification-and-numerical-adequacy/Conformity-assessment-_LPAR_ISO___IEC-Guide-98-4___-JCGM-106___2012_RPAR_/#conformity-assessment"},"/Using-the-library___-annotations-and-generated-indexes/The-generated-indexes/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Using-the-library___-annotations-and-generated-indexes--The-generated-indexes":{"contents":"What these tables index. Every table in this section is scoped to PropertyKindCalculus.Examples\n— the calculus's own worked examples, which are what this package has to index. They are not the\nsoil-moisture model: that model is a separate downstream repository, and its technical reference\nrenders the same eight tables over its own namespaces, populated with its real kinds and crossings.\n\nThe rows nonetheless read as soil-moisture material, and it is worth saying why rather than letting\nit look like a leak. The calculus's worked examples deliberately run on the domain that motivated\nit: a vegetation-attenuation forward model, Mironov coefficients, soil and soil-water as systems.\nThat is the point of a worked example — it has to be of something — and the alternative, examples\nover invented quantities, would demonstrate the machinery while hiding whether it survives contact\nwith a real model. The generic examples are here too, in the same tables: length and width as\ndistinct kinds, a blood group as a nominal scale, Mohs hardness as an ordinal one, the\nprinciple/method/procedure examination chain.\n\nSo the reading to avoid is that these tables document the model. They document the examples this\npackage ships, and they exist here for the same reason the boundary probe does: a derived table\nnobody renders is a table nobody notices has broken.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Using the library: annotations and generated indexes","header":"33.10. The generated indexes","id":"/Using-the-library___-annotations-and-generated-indexes/The-generated-indexes/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Using-the-library___-annotations-and-generated-indexes--The-generated-indexes"},"/Write-Once___-Correctly/Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum--Fused-forms-___-a-targeted-middle-ground":{"contents":"Between dispatching every operation eagerly and compiling the whole model there is a targeted lever: a\nfused form. On the eager carrier a single composed sub-expression of the model — say a two-way\nattenuation exp(c · x · y) — is several kernel launches and as many DRAM round-trips, one per\noperation. A fused form replaces that one recurring shape with a single kernel the carrier provides\ndirectly, computing the composition in one pass with its intermediates in registers. Crucially the\nfused form is a refinement of the composition, not an approximation of it: it is required to be\nbit-identical to the NumCarrier expression it stands in for — same operations, same association — so\nsubstituting it changes only how fast the shape is computed, never what it computes. Where a carrier\noffers no fused form for a shape, the model simply falls back to the plain composition; the write-once\nsource is unchanged either way.\n\nThis lever is domain-neutral and extensible. The forms are named by their mathematical shape, not by\nany application: the first is the scaled product exponential exp(c · x · y), of which a soil-moisture\nvegetation attenuation exp(−2 · b · ndvi) and a Beer–Lambert two-way extinction exp(−2 · κ · ℓ) are\ninstances — the domain reading lives downstream, in the science model that supplies the coefficient.\nFurther forms — an affine exponent exp(a + c · x) (Arrhenius, log-linear models), a Gaussian\nexp(−γ · x²) (radial-basis kernels), a numerically-stable logSumExp, a reciprocal square root — drop\nin the same way, each earned by a specific hot path rather than added speculatively.\n\nA fused form is the special case of the general fusion the next strategy performs. The megakernel\ncompiles the whole model into one kernel; a fused form hand-collapses one named shape within an\notherwise eager execution. It is the right tool when a single sub-expression dominates and the rest of\nthe model is content to run eagerly, or as the concrete kernel a compiled path targets for that shape.\nThe same discipline governs both: a fused form is trusted because it is a refinement of its composed\nspec, exactly as the megakernel is trusted because it is proved faithful to the recorded model.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Write Once, Correctly\u0009Go fast, automatically — the deployment spectrum","header":"18.3.4. Fused forms — a targeted middle ground","id":"/Write-Once___-Correctly/Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Write-Once___-Correctly--Go-fast___-automatically-___-the-deployment-spectrum--Fused-forms-___-a-targeted-middle-ground"}});